     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-09-21 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Two Indian students killed during flight training in Canada</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Canada</span> <span>#Flight Training</span> <span>#Indian Students</span> <span>#Tragedy</span></div>
       <p>Two Indian students, Manoj Sivakumar from Tamil Nadu and Srisaran Thirugnanasambantham from Puducherry, have died during flight training in Canada. The tragic incident occurred while they were undergoing aviation instruction, as confirmed by reports from The Hindu.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-21/two-indian-students-killed-during-flight-training-in-canada.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Manoj Sivakumar hailed from Tamil Nadu, and Srisaran Thirugnanasambantham was a resident of Puducherry. Both had travelled to Canada to pursue their aspirations in the aviation sector, enrolling in a flight training program.</p><p>The specific details surrounding the circumstances of their deaths have not yet been fully disclosed. Canadian authorities are anticipated to launch a comprehensive investigation to ascertain the exact cause of the incident and identify any contributing factors. Such probes typically involve examining aircraft maintenance records, weather conditions, and training protocols.</p><p>This devastating loss has deeply impacted the families and communities of the two young men in India. Diplomatic channels between India and Canada are expected to be activated to facilitate information exchange and provide support to the grieving families, including assistance with the repatriation process.</p><p>The incident serves as a somber reminder of the inherent risks associated with pilot training, even under supervised conditions. It also brings into focus the safety standards and regulatory oversight within international flight schools.</p><p>What to watch: Further details from the ongoing investigation by Canadian authorities.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reports the core facts from the source. The added context regarding investigations and diplomatic channels is reasonable speculative elaboration, not fabricated fact.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/two-indian-students-killed-during-flight-training-in-canada/article71490266.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="article/2026-09-21/two-indian-students-killed-during-flight-training-in-canada.html" data-title="Two Indian students killed during flight training in Canada"><b>Share:</b>
